%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 1afca256a50ee6842b710a96a363159c, type: 3}
  m_Name: assessment_03_safety
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ProjectSpark.UI.VisualTeaching.SparkAIAssessmentBank
  bankId: assessment_03_safety
  courseId: path_03_diagnostics
  displayName: 03 / Safety & Diagnosis Exam
  description: Test resistance heating, power, short-circuit concepts, solver reference
    signs and verified safe operation.
  passingPercent: 70
  items:
  - itemId: s1
    title: Power rating
    prompt: A resistor dissipates 0.50 W and is rated for 0.25 W. By what factor
      is it over its rated power?
    kind: 1
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    expectedNumber: 2
    numericTolerance: 0.01
    numericUnit: "\xD7"
    requiredTargetType: 0
    hint: Divide the dissipated power by the rated power.
    explanation: 0.50 / 0.25 = 2 times its rating.
  - itemId: s2
    title: Short-circuit concept
    prompt: Why can a wire that bypasses an intended load be dangerous?
    kind: 0
    choices:
    - It guarantees safe current
    - It may create excessive current
    - It always increases load resistance
    - It proves the load is operating
    correctChoice: 1
    expectedNumber: 0
    numericTolerance: 0.01
    numericUnit: 
    requiredTargetType: 0
    hint: Think about the effective resistance seen by the source.
    explanation: A low-resistance bypass may cause excessive current, depending on
      the actual source and circuit.
  - itemId: s3
    title: Signed current
    prompt: What does a negative current reading generally indicate in a circuit
      solver?
    kind: 0
    choices:
    - A burned circuit
    - Zero current
    - Flow opposite reference direction
    - Incorrect units
    correctChoice: 2
    expectedNumber: 0
    numericTolerance: 0.01
    numericUnit: 
    requiredTargetType: 0
    hint: A simulator chooses a reference direction.
    explanation: Negative current indicates flow opposite the solver's selected positive
      reference, not necessarily an electrical fault.
  - itemId: s4
    title: Resistor power
    prompt: "An ideal 100 \u03A9 resistor has 5 V across it. What power is dissipated
      in watts? Enter only the number."
    kind: 1
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    expectedNumber: 0.25
    numericTolerance: 0.005
    numericUnit: W
    requiredTargetType: 0
    hint: "P = V\xB2/R = 25/100."
    explanation: "P = 5\xB2/100 = 0.25 W."
  - itemId: s5
    title: 'Practical: Power range'
    prompt: In a level with a pending PowerRange target, satisfy it and obtain a
      new evaluator PASS.
    kind: 2
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    expectedNumber: 0
    numericTolerance: 0.01
    numericUnit: 
    requiredTargetType: 7
    hint: Check the objective binding and its configured minimum and maximum power.
    explanation: The official level evaluator passed the bound power-range objective
      after this attempt began.
